From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] follow the windows font smoothing setting in directwrite

Turning off "Smooth edges of screen fonts" in Windows makes GDI draw text
without antialiasing, but the DirectWrite engine never reads that setting,
only the smoothing type, ClearType or standard, and keeps antialiasing
every glyph. At a device pixel ratio of 1 that leaves all text smoothed
with the DirectWrite font database, the default since Qt 6.8, and some of
it with the GDI one, which since the fix for QTBUG-119995 creates
DirectWrite engines there for fonts that need a simulated style, such as
the italic of a font without an italic face.

Read SPI_GETFONTSMOOTHING with the other smoothing settings and, while it
is off and the device pixel ratio is 1, render aliased glyphs the way the
engine already does for QFont::NoAntialias, which also keeps glyphs on
whole pixels. Scaled text keeps antialiasing, as with Qt 5, because Qt
lays it out in logical pixels and the scale would stretch the rounded
advances. Color glyphs stay antialiased.

diff --git a/src/gui/text/windows/qwindowsfontdatabase.cpp b/src/gui/text/windows/qwindowsfontdatabase.cpp
index 0c6d9a31316..c878d660ead 100644
--- a/src/gui/text/windows/qwindowsfontdatabase.cpp
+++ b/src/gui/text/windows/qwindowsfontdatabase.cpp
@@ -74,6 +74,9 @@ QWindowsFontEngineData::QWindowsFontEngineData()
     UINT result = 0;
     if (SystemParametersInfo(SPI_GETFONTSMOOTHINGTYPE, 0, &result, 0))
         clearTypeEnabled = (result == FE_FONTSMOOTHINGCLEARTYPE);
+    BOOL smoothing = TRUE;
+    if (SystemParametersInfo(SPI_GETFONTSMOOTHING, 0, &smoothing, 0))
+        fontSmoothingEnabled = (smoothing != FALSE);
 
     const qreal gray_gamma = 2.31;
     for (int i=0; i<256; ++i)
